 { A lot of endgame play involves familiar concepts such as improving piece activity, strong pawn play, managing open & semi-open files, weak squares & outposts, etc. Since we have already covered this material, we won't be focusing on it here. Instead, we'll tackle four major topics: (1) Schematic thinking (2) Paralysis & Fortresses (3) Pawns in the endgame (4) The role of the king Schematic thinking is a mode of thinking that is invaluable in the endgame. It involves finding the right piece and pawn configuration, and the methods to reach it. The limited pieces in the endgame is one of the main reasons why the approach proves particularly effective. After this, we'll take a look at two concepts that are again very important in the endgame world with limited pieces on the board. They are paralysis (when one or more pieces are unable to move) as well as the concept of a fortress, a way of configuring the pieces in defense such that your opponent cannot make further progress in the position. In the second half of the chapter, we'll take a look at pawn formations and how they interact with minor pieces, particularly the bishop. In the final section, we'll build upon this knowledge by turning our focus to the piece that we have largely ignored throughout this course: the king! Hope you enjoy the chapter, let's dive in! } 1. { -KEY- } -- 
